Database migrations (Alembic).

The application runs `alembic upgrade head` at startup (see
create_db_and_tables in app/common/db_connection.py), so a normal deploy
needs nothing here. These are the manual commands.

  uv run alembic current                    # what the database is at
  uv run alembic history --verbose          # what exists
  uv run alembic upgrade head               # apply everything outstanding
  uv run alembic downgrade -1               # step back one
  uv run alembic upgrade head --sql         # print SQL instead of running it

Adding a migration
------------------
1. Change the model in app/models/.
2. uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what it does"
3. READ THE GENERATED FILE. Autogenerate is a first draft, not an answer.
   It routinely needs adjusting, and it cannot see two things at all:
     - Data. If a new constraint could be violated by rows already in the
       database, the migration has to repair them first, or it will fail on
       real data after the operator has taken the service down. See
       be83bce50e32 for the pattern.
     - Renames. A renamed column is emitted as a drop plus an add, which
       silently discards the data. Rewrite it as alter_column.
4. Apply it against a copy of a real database, not just an empty one.

SQLite notes
------------
SQLite cannot ALTER most things in place, so env.py sets
`render_as_batch=True` — Alembic rebuilds the table instead. Two
consequences:

  - Name every constraint explicitly. An unnamed constraint cannot be
    dropped again on SQLite, which makes downgrade() unwritable.
  - Batch operations are not transactional in the way you might assume.
    Back up the database file before upgrading anything you care about.

The database URL
----------------
alembic.ini deliberately has no sqlalchemy.url. env.py takes it from the
application's settings (conf/.env), so there is exactly one answer to "which
database" — otherwise it is entirely possible to migrate one file while the
app serves another.

Existing databases
------------------
A database created before Alembic existed has tables but no alembic_version.
Startup detects that and stamps it at the baseline revision (1ec329c28cf4,
which is exactly what the old create_all() produced) before upgrading, so an
existing deployment is not mistaken for a fresh one.
